 "scenes": [
  {
   "id": "intro",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Some problems have fast algorithms, and for others, after decades of effort, nobody has found one. Module 5 makes that difference precise. This first video defines the two classes everything else rests on: P and NP."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "head"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Complexity classes: P and NP"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Here are the seven stages."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "stages"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Tractable and intractable problems, decision problems, the class P, and the class NP."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "stages[0..=3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Then easy problems with hard twins, how P and NP relate, and an exam question."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "stages[4..=6]"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "tr",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 1: compare n cubed and 2 to the n for an input of size 60. n cubed is 216 thousand steps: a fraction of a millisecond."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 1 of 7: tractable or not"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "stages"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "tr"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "tr[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "2 to the 60 is over a billion billion steps: more than 36 years at a billion steps a second."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "tr[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "So we call a problem tractable if some algorithm solves it in polynomial time, O of n to the k for a constant k."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "tr[2]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Intractable problems have no known polynomial algorithm, and in practice we only know exponential ones."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "tr[3]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "polynomial = tractable"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Faster hardware does not rescue an exponential algorithm. A computer a thousand times faster lets a 2 to the n algorithm handle inputs only 10 larger, since 2 to the 10 is about a thousand. For n cubed, the input can grow ten times."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "tr[1]"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "dp",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 2: complexity theory studies decision problems, whose answer is yes or no. Shortest path becomes: is there a path with at most k edges?"
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 2 of 7: decision problems"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "tr"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "dp"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "dp[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The travelling salesman becomes: is there a tour of cost at most k? The largest clique becomes: is there a clique of size k?"
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "dp[1..=2]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "This loses nothing that matters. If the yes-or-no version is already hard, the optimisation version, which answers it, is at least as hard."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "dp[3]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "decision problems: answer yes or no"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "p",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 3: P is the class of decision problems that can be solved in polynomial time."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 3 of 7: the class P"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "dp"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "pc"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "pc[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Almost everything in this course so far is in P: sorting, shortest paths, spanning trees, maximum flow, two-colouring, the dynamic programs."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "pc[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "P is robust: a polynomial algorithm that calls a polynomial subroutine polynomially often is still polynomial."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "pc[2]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "n to the 100 is technically in P, but natural problems in P almost always have small exponents, so P is a good working definition of efficient."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "pc[3]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "P: solvable in polynomial time"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "np",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 4: consider the Hamiltonian cycle problem: is there a cycle that visits every vertex exactly once? No polynomial algorithm is known."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 4 of 7: the class NP"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "pc"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "g"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "np"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "np[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "But suppose someone hands us an answer: the order 1, 2, 4, 5, 3. That is a certificate."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "np[1]"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "g.v1"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "g.v2",
       "delay": 0.15
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "g.v4",
       "delay": 0.3
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "g.v5",
       "delay": 0.45
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "g.v3",
       "delay": 0.6
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Checking it is easy. Every vertex appears once. And each consecutive pair is an edge: 1 to 2, 2 to 4, 4 to 5, 5 to 3, and 3 back to 1. Yes: a Hamiltonian cycle."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "np[2]"
      },
      {
       "action": "mark",
       "target": "g.v1->v2"
      },
      {
       "action": "mark",
       "target": "g.v2->v4",
       "delay": 0.15
      },
      {
       "action": "mark",
       "target": "g.v4->v5",
       "delay": 0.3
      },
      {
       "action": "mark",
       "target": "g.v3->v5",
       "delay": 0.45
      },
      {
       "action": "mark",
       "target": "g.v1->v3",
       "delay": 0.6
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "A wrong certificate, like 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, fails at once: 3 and 4 are not joined. Checking takes O of n squared time at most."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "g.v3"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "g.v4",
       "delay": 0.3
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "NP is the class of decision problems whose yes-answers have certificates that can be checked in polynomial time. The name means nondeterministic polynomial: a machine that could guess the certificate would solve the problem fast."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "np[3..=4]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "NP: a yes-answer can be checked fast"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "tw",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 5: hard problems often look almost exactly like easy ones. Shortest simple path is in P; longest simple path is NP-complete."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 5 of 7: hard twins"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "g"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "np"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "tw"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "tw[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "An Euler tour, using every edge once, is easy to decide. A Hamiltonian cycle, using every vertex once, is hard."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "tw[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Satisfiability with two literals per clause is in P; with three it is NP-complete. Two-colouring is BFS; three-colouring is hard."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "tw[2..=3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Trying every order of the vertices to find a Hamiltonian cycle means trying n minus 1 factorial over 2 orders: 12 for 5 vertices, but over 10 to the 16 for 20. That is why a certificate, which skips the search, is so valuable."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "tw[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "And the minimum spanning tree is greedy and fast, while the travelling salesman, which also connects every city cheaply, is hard. A small change in the question can cross the line."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "tw[4]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "small changes, huge difference"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "pn",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 6: every problem in P is in NP. If you can solve it fast, you can check a yes-answer by just solving it."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 6 of 7: P versus NP"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "tw"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "pn"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "pn[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Is every problem in NP also in P? Nobody knows. It is the most famous open question in computer science."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "pn[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Most researchers believe P is not NP: that finding an answer is genuinely harder than checking one. A proof either way would earn a million-dollar Millennium Prize."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "pn[2..=3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The next videos meet the NP-complete problems: the hardest in NP. A polynomial algorithm for any one of them would give one for all of NP."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "pn[4]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "P ⊆ NP; P = NP is open"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "exam",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Now an exam question."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Exam question"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "pn"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "q"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "q[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Give a certificate and its checking cost, decide whether a negative question is obviously in NP, and classify a path problem. Pause and work it out."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "q[1..=2]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "a: for subset sum, the certificate is the chosen subset. Add its numbers and compare with t: linear time. So subset sum is in NP."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "q"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "qa"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "qa[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "b: to prove a graph has no Hamiltonian cycle, what short certificate could we hand over? None is known. Such complements of NP problems form the class co-NP, which is not known to equal NP."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "qa[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "c: breadth-first search answers it in O of V plus E. So it is in P, and therefore also in NP."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "qa[2]"
      },
      {
       "action": "mark",
       "target": "qa[2][2]",
       "delay": 0.5
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The usual slip: thinking NP means not polynomial. NP means checkable in polynomial time, and it includes all of P."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
